  "section_header": "EFI SYSTEM — Electronic Control System",
  "title": "Electronic Controlled Unit (ECU)",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "INSPECTION OF ECU"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. MEASURE VOLTAGE OF ECU"
    },
    {
      "type": "notice",
      "text": "The EFI circuit can be checked by measuring the resistance and voltage at the wiring connectors of the ECU."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Check the voltages at the wiring connectors."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Remove the glove box.",
        "Turn on the ignition switch.",
        "Measure the voltage at each terminal."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "index": 1,
      "description": "Illustration showing a voltmeter connected to ECU wiring connectors. The voltmeter is shown on the left with leads connecting to the ECU unit on the right. The ECU has two connectors labeled E1 and +B.",
      "labels": [
        "Voltmeter",
        "ECU",
        "E1",
        "+B"
      ],
      "bbox": {
        "top": 22,
        "left": 3,
        "width": 45,
        "height": 25
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "notice",
      "text": "1. Perform all voltage measurements with the connectors connected.\n2. Verify that the battery voltage is 11V or above when the ignition switch is ON."
    },
